1|Photosynthesis is how a plant uses sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to make sugar and release oxygen.\n2|It happens in chloroplasts in the leaves.\n3|It is the base of many food chains.\n4|A decorative color with no light capture\n5|A green pigment in leaves that takes in light and allows photosynthesis\n6|Water alone holding all the plant's energy\n7|Soil minerals with no help from pigments\n8|Chlorophyll is the green material in leaves that takes in sunlight so the plant can make food.\n9|It is found in chloroplasts.\n10|What is the role of roots in a plant?\n11|Roots capture sunlight for all parts of the plant.\n12|Roots absorb water and nutrients from the soil to feed the plant.\n13|Roots are where animals find shelter during cold weather.\n14|Roots release seeds into the surrounding soil.\n15|Roots absorb water and nutrients from the soil. These travel upward through the stem and into the rest of the plant, supporting its growth.\n16|Roots grow underground and anchor the plant in the soil.\n17|Water and minerals travel from the roots upward through the stem.\n18|What is the role of the stem in a plant?\n19|The stem produces food for the plant by using sunlight.\n20|The stem supports the plant and carries water and nutrients to other parts.\n21|The stem is where animals find shelter during winter.\n22|The stem absorbs carbon dioxide directly from the soil.\n23|The stem supports the plant and acts like a pipe, carrying water and nutrients from the roots to the leaves and other parts of the plant.\n24|The stem connects the roots to the leaves.\n25|Water absorbed by roots travels through the stem to reach the leaves.\n26|What is the role of leaves in a plant?\n27|Leaves help animals see where to find food.\n28|Leaves capture sunlight for photosynthesis and produce oxygen as a result.\n29|Leaves act as the lungs for animals that live inside the plant.\n30|Leaves carry water through the plant instead of the stem.\n31|Leaves are the plant's food factory. They capture sunlight and use it, along with water and carbon dioxide, to make food through photosynthesis. Oxygen is released as a byproduct.\n32|Leaves contain chlorophyll, which captures sunlight.\n33|Photosynthesis produces food for the plant and releases oxygen into the air.\n34|Fruit tissue with no embryo that can grow\n35|A plant part with an embryo that can grow into a new plant\n36|A flower structure with no embryo for sprouting\n37|A dry leaf that cannot start new growth\n38|Seeds come from flowers.\n39|A seed has what is needed to start a new plant.\n40|What does a fruit do for a plant?\n41|A fruit makes food to help the flower bloom each season.\n42|A fruit contains and protects seeds, helping them spread to new places.\n43|A fruit is a part of the leaf that stores water for the plant.\n44|A fruit grows directly from the plant's roots underground.\n45|The fruit develops around the seeds to protect them. Animals eat the fruit and carry the seeds to new locations, helping the plant spread.\n46|Fruits help with seed dispersal by attracting animals.\n47|Animals eat the fruit and carry the seeds away from the parent plant.\n48|What happens when a seed gets enough water, warmth, and light?\n49|The entire plant, including all its roots and flowers, appears instantly.\n50|The seed germinates and begins to grow into a new plant.\n51|Water fills the seed but nothing else happens.\n52|The root dries out and falls off the seed.\n53|When conditions are right — enough water, warmth, and sometimes light — a seed germinates. A small root and shoot grow out of the seed and develop into a new plant.\n54|Germination is the process of a seed beginning to sprout and grow.\n55|Seeds need water, warmth, and sometimes light to germinate successfully.\n56|How does a fruit form on a plant?\n57|A seed falls to the ground and turns directly into a fruit.\n58|A fruit develops from the flower after pollination.\n59|Fruit grows inside the leaves when they get enough sunlight.\n60|Water flowing up through the root forms the fruit directly.\n61|After a flower is pollinated, the petals fall away and the base of the flower swells and grows into a fruit that contains seeds.\n62|Pollination is the transfer of pollen that fertilizes the flower.\n63|The fertilized flower develops into a fruit that holds and protects the seeds.\n64|What are the states of matter?\n65|A name only for the solid state, with no liquids or gases\n66|Solid, liquid, and gas—different forms matter can take\n67|A description of liquids with no solid or gas\n68|Only the gas state at every temperature\n69|States of matter are the forms matter can take: solid (hard shape), liquid (flows), or gas (spreads out).\n70|Water can exist in all three states.\n71|Temperature can change the state of matter.\n72|What is melting?\n73|Heating with no change of state\n74|A change from solid to liquid\n75|Cooling that makes ice instead of water\n76|Freezing a liquid into a solid\n77|Melting is the change from solid to liquid—for example, ice turning into water when heated.\n78|Melting happens when temperature rises.\n79|Every material has its own melting point.\n80|What is freezing?\n81|Cooling with no change to a solid\n82|A change from liquid to solid\n83|Heating that melts ice into water\n84|Melting a gas into a liquid\n85|Freezing is the change from liquid to solid—for example, water turning into ice when cooled.\n86|Freezing happens when temperature drops.\n87|Every material has its own freezing point.\n88|What is evaporation?\n89|Heating with no liquid turning into air\n90|A change from liquid to gas\n91|Cooling that gathers water vapor into liquid\n92|Only melting a solid into a liquid\n93|Evaporation is the change from liquid to gas—for example, water turning into water vapor when heated.\n94|Evaporation increases as temperature rises.\n95|You can see it when water boils.\n96|What is condensation?\n97|Cooling with no liquid droplets forming\n98|A change from gas to liquid\n99|Heating that fully spreads water vapor away\n100|Only melting ice into water\n101|Condensation is the change from gas to liquid—for example, water vapor turning into water droplets when cooled.\n102|Condensation happens when temperature drops.\n103|You can see it on cool windows when vapor turns to droplets.\n104|Which description fits an electrical or heat conductor?\n105|A material that blocks heat and stops it from moving.\n106|A material that allows heat or electricity to pass through it easily.\n107|Wood — it is the best conductor of electricity.\n108|Plastic — it conducts electricity better than any metal.\n109|Conductors let heat or electricity move through them easily. Many metals are good conductors; wood and plastic usually are not.\n110|Metals such as copper and iron are common conductors.\n111|Insulators slow down or block the flow of heat or electricity.\n112|What is an insulating material?\n113|Damp wood that still conducts electricity well\n114|A pure metal with free electrons\n115|Thin glass as a strong electricity conductor\n116|An insulator does not carry heat or electricity well. Wood, plastic, and rubber are examples.\n117|Insulators help keep heat in or out.\n118|They are important for safety.\n119|Purified iron that never responds to a magnet\n120|A material that is attracted to a magnet\n121|Copper that responds to a magnet like iron\n122|Pure gold that sticks strongly to a permanent magnet\n123|A magnetic material is attracted to a magnet. Iron, nickel, and cobalt are examples.\n124|Not all metals are magnetic.\n125|Opaque glass you cannot see through\n126|Smoked plastic that does not transmit light\n127|Dirty water that fully blocks vision\n128|A transparent material lets light pass through so you can see through it—like clear glass or clean water.\n129|A synthetic polymer with no link to plants\n130|A mineral salt with no direct link to living remains\n131|Pure sandstone with no living leftovers\n132|Organic materials come from living things—wood, cotton, and wool are examples.\n133|Some are better for the environment.\n134|How is a planet different from a star like the Sun?\n135|A planet is any bright sparkle seen only at night.\n136|A moon is exactly the same thing as a planet.\n137|A planet produces its own light just like the Sun does.\n138|Stars make their own light and heat. Planets orbit stars and shine mainly by reflecting light.\n139|The Sun is a star.\n140|What best describes a planet in our solar system?\n141|A planet produces its own light, just like the Sun.\n142|A large body that orbits the Sun and does not produce its own light.\n143|A moon that orbits Earth.\n144|A tiny object smaller than a comet.\n145|Planets are large bodies that orbit the Sun. They do not generate their own light the way stars do — instead, they reflect sunlight. Earth, Mars, and Jupiter are all examples of planets in our solar system.\n146|There are eight planets in our solar system, ranging from small rocky worlds to giant gas planets.\n147|A planet must be large enough for its gravity to pull it into a roughly round shape.\n148|What is the Moon?\n149|The Moon is a large planet that orbits other large planets.\n150|The Moon is a rocky body that orbits Earth.\n151|The Moon is a small object that orbits the Sun directly on its own.\n152|The Moon is a cloud of water vapor surrounding Earth.\n153|The Moon is Earth's only natural satellite. It is a rocky body that orbits Earth about once every 28 days and reflects sunlight.\n154|The Moon reflects sunlight, which is why we can see it at night.\n155|A sparkling body that makes its own light\n156|A rock in space that orbits the Sun\n157|A heavy body like Earth in orbit\n158|A satellite that orbits the Moon\n159|Precipitation with no evaporation or flow back to the sea\n160|The process in which water moves among ocean, land, and atmosphere\n161|Snow stuck in one place with no melting or evaporating\n162|Fixed clouds with no precipitation to the ground\n163|In the water cycle, water moves among ocean, land, and air through evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.\n164|It is an ongoing process.\n165|Water evaporates, forms clouds, and falls as rain or snow.\n166|What are clouds?\n167|Dry air with no visible droplets\n168|Tiny water droplets or ice crystals in the atmosphere\n169|Dust alone with no moisture\n170|Fast wind with no gathered droplets\n171|Clouds are tiny water droplets or ice crystals in the air. When they get heavy, rain or snow can fall.\n172|There are many kinds of clouds.\n173|What does rain do for the Earth?\n174|Rain makes the soil harder and prevents water from reaching plant roots.\n175|Rain brings fresh water to the soil, which plants and animals need to survive.\n176|Rain only creates puddles on the surface and has no other purpose.\n177|Rain is water that rises upward from the soil into the clouds.\n178|Rain is an important part of the water cycle. It brings fresh water to the land, filling rivers, lakes, and soil so that plants, animals, and people can use it.\n179|Rain is a key stage in the water cycle.\n180|Water from rain is absorbed by soil and used by plants and animals.\n181|What is snow?\n182|Thick ice on grass with no fall from the sky\n183|Ice crystals that fall from clouds when it is very cold\n184|Warm rain with no ice at all\n185|Heavy hailstones always on the ground\n186|Snow is made of ice crystals that fall from clouds in very cold weather. Each snowflake has its own pattern.\n187|Snow falls when temperatures are below freezing.\n188|Snow is important for climate and water supply.\n189|How does heat move?\n190|Heat stays in one place and never moves on its own.\n191|Heat moves from warmer areas to cooler areas.\n192|Heat can only move through water and nothing else.\n193|Rain is what causes heat to move upward into the sky.\n194|Heat always flows from a warmer area to a cooler area. This movement of energy is called heat transfer.\n195|Heat transfer happens in three ways: conduction, convection, and radiation.\n196|On a sunny day, warm air rises because heat moves from the warm ground into the cooler air above.\n197|Air with no particles or gases at all\n198|Harmful materials in the air that hurt health and the environment\n199|Rain that always washes dirt away with no leftovers\n200|Sunlight that blocks gases from entering